3. Next, we define the binary_search function. This function takes three parameters: an array of integers, the size of the array, and the target integer we're searching for.
4. Inside the function, we initialize two variables, left and right, to represent the start and end of the search range. Initially, left is set to 0 and right is set to the last index of the array.
5. We then enter a while loop that continues as long as the search range is not empty, i.e., as long as left is less than or equal to right.
6. Inside the loop, we calculate the middle index of the current search range. If the value at this index is equal to the target, we've found the target and return its index.
7. If the value at the middle index is less than the target, we know the target must be in the right half of the current search range. So, we update left to be one more than the middle index.
8. If the value at the middle index is greater than the target, we know the target must be in the left half of the current search range. So, we update right to be one less than the middle index.
9. If we exit the loop without finding the target, it means the target is not in the array. In this case, we return -1.
10. In the main function, we define an array and a target value, and call the binary_search function with these values.
11. We then print the result. If the result is not -1, it means the target was found at that index. Otherwise, it means the target was not found in the array.

#include <iostream>

// Function to perform binary search on a sorted array
int binary_search(int arr[], int size, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = size - 1;

    // Continue searching while the range is not empty
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid; // Target found, return its index
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1; // Search in the right half
        } else {
            right = mid - 1; // Search in the left half
        }
    }

    return -1; // Target not found, return -1
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15};
    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int target = 7;

    int result = binary_search(arr, size, target);

    if (result != -1) {
        std::cout << "Element found at index: " << result << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Element not found in the array" << std::endl;
    }

